<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Benito Juárez</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1806">1806</a>–<a href="/years/1872">1872</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/19th-century">19th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Benito Juárez was a Zapotec lawyer who served as President of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mexico">Mexico</a> from 1858 until his death in 1872. A leader of the liberal Reform, he separated church and state and defended the 1857 constitution in the Reform War. He led resistance to the French intervention and the empire of Maximilian, who was executed in 1867.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/benito-juarez.jpg" alt="Benito Juárez" width="320" height="441" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Unknown photographer · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Photograph_of_Benito_Juarez.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Liberals and conservatives had fought the War of the Reform of 1858–1861 over the Constitution of 1857, which curbed the privileges of the Catholic Church and the army, and when <b>Benito Juárez</b> suspended payments on foreign debt in 1861, France invaded with the support of Mexican conservatives.</blockquote><a href="/empire/mexico">Mexico · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>The Constitution of the United States inspired constitution-makers well beyond those who copied its presidency, from <b>Benito Juárez</b> in Mexico to José Rizal in the Philippines and Sun Yat-sen in China, although since the 1980s new constitutions have more often looked elsewhere.</blockquote><a href="/government/presidential-democracy">Presidential democracy · Impact &amp; Legacy</a></li><li><blockquote>Conservatives revolted, and <b>Benito Juárez</b> won the Reform War of 1858 to 1861.</blockquote><a href="/state/second-federal-republic-of-mexico">Second Federal Republic of Mexico</a></li><li><blockquote>His power depended on French troops fighting <b>Benito Juárez's</b> republicans across the country.</blockquote><a href="/state/second-mexican-empire">Second Mexican Empire</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in San Pablo Guelatao, in what is now <a href="/countries/mexico">Mexico</a>. Died in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mexico-city">Mexico City</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/mexico">Mexico</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=25.45,-103.24&amp;year=1848" aria-label="San Pablo Guelatao on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="1816 1228 900 540" role="img" aria-label="Map: the birthplace of Benito Juárez"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M2266 1498h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M2266 1498h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=25.45,-103.24&amp;year=1848">What was here in 1848? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
